## Repository Structure
The project lives in a single GitHub repository named good-night-games. Both the frontend and backend are subdirectories within it. This keeps all Good Night Games code in one place, allows shared TypeScript types between frontend and backend, and means one CI pipeline covers both sides.
```
good-night-games/                  ← GitHub repo root
├── frontend/                      ← Next.js app (Survey Showdown UI)
│   └── src/
│       ├── app/                   ← App Router pages, globals.css (design tokens + animations)
│       ├── components/            ← survey-showdown/, shared/
│       ├── contexts/              ← AuthContext (session, config, referrals)
│       ├── hooks/                 ← useAuth, useProductConfig, useTokenBalance
│       └── lib/api/               ← typed API clients (JWT via Supabase session)
├── backend/                       ← Hono API, Prisma schema, migrations
│   ├── prisma/                    ← schema + SQL migrations
│   ├── src/routes/                ← product + /api/survey-showdown/*
│   └── docs/                      ← Supabase routines runbooks
├── shared/                        ← Optional shared TypeScript types (add when needed)
├── scripts/                       ← prepare-release.sh / .ps1 (semver + GitHub releases)
├── .github/workflows/ci.yml       ← backend tsc + Vitest on push/PR
└── README.md
```
Each subdirectory has its own `package.json` and dependencies installed independently. There is no root-level `package.json` — run `npm ci` inside `frontend/` or `backend/` as needed. Railway uses `backend/` as root directory; Vercel builds `frontend/`.

